The data in that dashboard is 4 queries, with the CSV of `category,value`. Category for all rows in all queries is the same - `apple`. Each query has 2 rows of data, either a value counting down from 4, up from 1.

| Query1  | Query 2 | Query 3 | Query 4 |
| ------- | ------- | ------- | ------- |
| 4  | 3  | 2 | 1  |
| 1 | 2  | 3  | 4  |

In 9.5.2, the result of the join to 'category' has the last value from each query. There is only one row in the table.

<img width="862" height="135" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/4155b303-a433-409c-8459-ea49dea5066f" />

In `main`, the join returns all possible permutations of the data, so 2^4, 16 rows.

<img width="1229" height="530" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/5c46a7cb-36d3-498f-9b54-6ce3b5a47c36" />

Obviously 16 rows is not that big of a deal, but in the case of large data sets, an inner join ballooning the data size this much is an issue.
